/* 各卷写作参考范文：小作文 / 大作文，按 paper id 索引 */
window.__ESSAYS__ = {
 "2006-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am a college student writing to inquire about the possibility of sponsoring a child through Project Hope. I have long been concerned about education in remote areas and would like to offer financial aid to a pupil whose family can barely afford tuition and textbooks.\n\nI would prefer a girl aged between eight and ten from a mountainous area in western China, since girls there are more likely to drop out of school. I plan to cover her tuition and book fees, about 600 yuan a year, until she finishes junior high school. Meanwhile, I would be grateful if I could keep in touch with her by letter so as to follow her progress.\n\nI would appreciate it if you could help me find a suitable candidate. Thank you for your time.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The two photos present a striking scene that has provoked wide discussion. In the first one, a young man has painted the name \"Beckham\" across his face; in the second, another fan has paid 300 yuan for a haircut identical to his idol's. For these admirers, copying a star's appearance seems to be the shortest way to express their enthusiasm.\n\nSuch behaviour reflects a worrying tendency among the young. Admittedly, admiring an outstanding person is natural, and a proper idol can inspire us to work harder. Yet when worship is reduced to face paint and a fashionable hairstyle, it turns into blind imitation. What these fans copy is the surface of success rather than the qualities that produced it: diligence, self-discipline and years of practice.\n\nIn my view, the young should learn to admire with a clear head. Instead of spending money and time on appearances, we would do better to ask what makes an idol admirable and to put that spirit into our own study and work. Only in this way can admiration become a source of strength rather than a passing fever."
 },
 "2007-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am a regular user of the university library and I am writing to offer several suggestions for improving its service.\n\nFirst, the opening hours could be extended to eleven o'clock at night during the examination weeks, as many students find it hard to get a seat in the daytime. Second, the online catalogue should be updated more promptly; quite often a book is listed as available while it is actually on loan. Third, it would be helpful to set up a small discussion room on the third floor, where group study is allowed without disturbing others.\n\nI hope you will consider these proposals, and I would be glad to offer further help if needed.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is vividly shown in the drawing, a young man is standing in front of a football goal, ready to take a penalty kick. What makes the scene unusual is his distorted self-image: he appears as a giant, while the goal shrinks to the size of a toy. The caption tells us that he is a university graduate who believes he is fully prepared for whatever lies ahead.\n\nThe picture carries an evident message about self-confidence. Confidence is the first step towards success, for it gives us the courage to seize opportunities and the strength to persist when difficulties arise. A person who trusts his own ability is more likely to speak up in an interview, to propose a bold plan, and to recover quickly from failure. Without it, talent often remains buried and chances slip away unnoticed.\n\nHowever, the drawing also warns us against overconfidence. Those who overestimate themselves may refuse to learn, ignore advice and end up frustrated. Genuine confidence grows out of solid preparation rather than wishful thinking. Only when we know both our strengths and our limits can we face the goal calmly and score."
 },
 "2008-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Bob,\n\nI have just come back from Canada and, while unpacking, I found a music CD in my luggage, the one you lent me before I left. I am terribly sorry that I forgot to return it.\n\nTo make up for my carelessness, I will send it back by international express tomorrow and cover the postage myself. If you prefer, I can also buy a new copy here and post it together with a small gift. Please let me know which you would like.\n\nAgain, please accept my sincere apology for the inconvenience I have caused, and thank you for your kindness during my stay.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the drawing, two disabled men, each of whom has only one leg, are walking forward with their arms around each other's shoulders. With their crutches left behind, they move as steadily as anyone else, because each lends the other the leg he lacks.\n\nSimple as the picture is, it conveys a profound truth: cooperation makes the impossible possible. In a world where tasks grow ever more complex, no individual can master everything. What one person lacks, another may supply; what one cannot see, another may point out. The same logic applies to scientific research, to business, and to the ordinary life of a class or a family. Where people join hands, resources are pooled, weaknesses are offset, and the whole becomes greater than the sum of its parts.\n\nTherefore we should learn to cooperate rather than compete blindly. Opening our minds to others' strengths and offering our own is not a sign of weakness but of wisdom. As the saying goes, one person may travel fast, but a group travels far."
 },
 "2009-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing to express my concern about the \"white pollution\" that still surrounds us. Although plastic bags have been restricted for years, they remain everywhere: in supermarkets, at food stalls, and even drifting across the campus lawn.\n\nIn my opinion, the present measures are too mild. First, shops should be required to charge a small fee for every plastic bag, so that customers begin to weigh convenience against cost. Second, the authorities could organise monthly clean-up campaigns and publish the results, which would put pressure on the worst districts. Third, we citizens should form the habit of carrying cloth bags and sorting our rubbish at home.\n\nI hope our newspaper can help raise public awareness of this issue, for a clean city depends on every one of us.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is illustrated in the cartoon, an enormous spider web fills the room, and a man sits at its centre, facing a computer screen. The web connects him with every corner of the world, yet the room around him is empty and silent.\n\nThe drawing reveals a paradox of the information age. On the one hand, the Internet has broken geographical barriers: a message crosses the ocean in a second, and a student in a small town can attend lectures given by famous professors. On the other hand, the same technology may quietly separate us from the people beside us. Many of us have seen friends at one table, each buried in a phone, saying nothing to one another.\n\nWhat we need, therefore, is a balance. We should enjoy the convenience the web offers without letting it replace a real conversation, a shared meal, or a walk with a friend. Technology is a bridge, not a substitute for human warmth."
 },
 "2010-y1": {
  "small": "Notice\n\nVolunteers are needed for the International Conference on Globalization, which will be held in our university from 15 to 18 May. Twenty volunteers will be recruited to help with registration, guidance and interpretation.\n\nApplicants should be postgraduates with a good command of English, a strong sense of responsibility and good communication skills. Those who have previous experience in similar events are preferred.\n\nAnyone interested is asked to send an application form to the office of the Postgraduates' Association before 30 April, or email us at pa@university.edu.cn. A short interview will be arranged next week.\n\nThe Postgraduates' Association",
  "big": "As is vividly depicted in the picture, a hot pot is boiling in the centre of the table, and into it have been thrown an astonishing variety of ingredients. Along with the traditional Chinese food, we can see Confucius and Shakespeare, Peking Opera and Hollywood films, kung fu and the theory of relativity. The caption reads, \"Cultural hot pot, delicious and nutritious.\"\n\nThis humorous image captures the essence of cultural exchange in our age. Cultures are not sealed boxes; they meet, mix and enrich one another. The hot pot itself is a perfect metaphor: each ingredient keeps its own flavour, yet together they produce a taste that none of them could create alone. In the same way, an open culture absorbs what is useful from others without losing its own identity, and grows stronger in the process.\n\nFor us as individuals, the lesson is equally clear. To live in a globalised world, we should be tolerant and curious, willing to taste what is unfamiliar and proud enough to share what is our own. A culture that dares to open its pot will always have something new to offer."
 },
 "2011-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Wang Fang,\n\nYou asked me to recommend a film, and I would like to suggest The Pursuit of Happyness, starring Will Smith.\n\nThe film tells the true story of a father who loses his job and his flat, yet never gives up. While raising his young son alone, he takes an unpaid internship and studies at night. Its power lies not in grand scenes but in small, honest details that make you believe in human resilience.\n\nI recommend it especially because we are both facing a demanding year. Whenever I feel discouraged, that film reminds me that hardship is temporary and that effort is never wasted.\n\nI hope you will enjoy it as much as I did.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is shown in the drawing, two tourists are enjoying a boat trip on a lake, laughing and chatting. Behind them, however, empty bottles, plastic bags and food wrappers are floating on the water, turning beautiful scenery into a rubbish dump.\n\nThe picture points to a familiar contradiction: the very people who travel to admire nature are often the ones who damage it. Tourism brings income and joy, but mass tourism without responsibility costs far more than it earns. A lake that takes thousands of years to form can be ruined in a single holiday season.\n\nTo avoid such a tragedy, efforts must be made on several fronts. Governments should set visitor limits and build proper waste facilities; travel agencies should educate tourists before departure; and each of us should carry our rubbish away, so that we leave nothing behind but footprints. Only when pleasure and responsibility go hand in hand can the scenery remain for those who come after us."
 },
 "2012-y1": {
  "small": "Dear friends,\n\nOn behalf of the Students' Union, I would like to extend a warm welcome to all of you. We are delighted that you have chosen our university, and we hope your stay here will be both rewarding and enjoyable.\n\nTo help you settle in, here are a few suggestions. First, apply for a campus card as soon as possible; it is used for the library, the dining halls and the gym. Second, join at least one student club, where you can make local friends and practise your Chinese. Third, keep a map of the campus on your phone, because our buildings are numbered in a slightly confusing way.\n\nIf you have any questions, please feel free to contact us. We look forward to seeing you on campus.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the drawing, a bottle has been knocked over and half of the water has spilled onto the ground. Facing the same accident, the two men react in opposite ways: one covers his face and sighs, \"It's all gone!\", while the other bends down, smiles and says, \"There is still half left.\"\n\nThe picture illustrates how attitude shapes our experience. The pessimistic man fixes his eyes on what has been lost, so the accident becomes a disaster; the optimistic one notices what remains, so the same accident becomes a new starting point. Neither of them can change the spilled water, but their interpretations lead them in opposite directions.\n\nLife rarely goes exactly as we plan. Examinations are failed, plans are cancelled, and chances slip away. At such moments, being optimistic does not mean ignoring the loss; it means refusing to be defined by it. Those who can look at a broken bottle and still see half a glass of water are the ones who keep moving forward, and who eventually turn setbacks into strength."
 },
 "2013-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Professor Smith,\n\nI am writing on behalf of the Students' Union to invite you to be a judge at our English Speech Contest, which will be held at 7 p.m. on 20 May in the lecture hall of the Foreign Languages Building.\n\nThe contest will last about two hours. Twelve contestants will speak for five minutes each on the theme \"Youth and Responsibility\", and afterwards three prizes will be awarded. As you have taught public speaking for many years, your comments would be of great value to our students.\n\nWe would be honoured if you could join us. Please let me know your decision at your earliest convenience.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is vividly shown in the picture, a group of graduates stand at a crossroads, each path leading in a different direction. Some hold offers from companies, others hold admission letters from universities, and a few simply stare at the signposts, uncertain which way to take.\n\nThe drawing captures a dilemma that almost every graduate faces. On the one hand, having choices is a privilege; it means that years of study have opened doors. On the other hand, too many doors can paralyse us. Weighing salary against interest, stability against adventure, and parents' expectations against our own dreams, many young people postpone the decision until opportunity quietly passes.\n\nIn my view, there is no perfect path, only a path that suits us at a particular moment. Instead of searching for a choice that carries no risk, we should ask what we truly care about and what we are willing to pay for it. Once the direction is chosen, we should walk it with confidence, for it is action, not hesitation, that turns a crossroads into a journey."
 },
 "2014-y1": {
  "small": "Dear President,\n\nI am writing to suggest some ways of improving the physical condition of our students. Many of my classmates spend most of the day sitting in classrooms or in front of computers, and a recent medical check showed that nearly a third of us are short-sighted or overweight.\n\nFirst, the gym could stay open until ten in the evening, since most students are free only after their last class. Second, the university might organise a monthly sports day, with events such as relay races, basketball and rope skipping, so that exercise becomes a habit rather than an obligation. Third, the canteens could offer more low-sugar and low-fat dishes.\n\nI hope these suggestions will be of some use, and I would be glad to help in any way I can.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the picture, the relationship between parents and children is presented in two touching scenes. In one, a young couple holds the hands of their little daughter as she takes her first steps; in the other, the same daughter, now grown up, supports her aged parents as they walk slowly down the street.\n\nSimple as the drawing is, it conveys a profound message about family love. The first scene shows care flowing downwards: parents give their time, patience and strength without asking for anything in return. The second shows gratitude flowing back: when the parents grow old and weak, the child becomes the hand that they once held. This cycle of giving and returning is the warmest part of human life.\n\nNowadays, however, many young people are so busy with work that they can offer their parents little more than a phone call. The drawing reminds us that material support is not enough; what the elderly need most is company. Let us remember who held our hands when we could not walk, and be ready to hold theirs when they can no longer walk alone."
 },
 "2015-y1": {
  "small": "Dear club members,\n\nFor our reading session this Friday, I would like to recommend a book that has changed the way I look at my own life: Man's Search for Meaning by Viktor Frankl.\n\nThe author, a psychiatrist who survived a concentration camp, argues that the last human freedom is the freedom to choose one's attitude. He does not offer easy comfort; instead, with quiet details, he shows how people find meaning even in extreme suffering.\n\nI recommend it because we are all preparing for examinations and sometimes feel overwhelmed. The book does not tell us how to pass a test, but it helps us understand why we keep trying. I believe it will spark a lively discussion on Friday.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is vividly shown in the picture, four friends are sitting around a table in a restaurant. Instead of talking to one another, each of them is staring at a smart phone, and the table is filled with an awkward silence.\n\nThe picture reflects a social phenomenon of our time. Smart phones have brought us enormous convenience: news, payment, maps and old friends are all within a single screen. Yet the same devices are quietly stealing something precious, namely the attention we used to give to the people in front of us. A dinner that should have been a conversation becomes four people sharing a table but living in four separate worlds.\n\nIn my view, technology should serve human contact rather than replace it. It is not necessary to abandon our phones; it is enough to set a simple rule, such as putting them away during meals or looking up when someone speaks. The warmth of a real conversation is something no screen can reproduce, and it is worth the small effort of lifting our eyes."
 },
 "2016-y1": {
  "small": "Notice\n\nTo help our newly-enrolled international students make full use of the library, the following information may be useful.\n\nThe library is open from 8 a.m. to 10 p.m. on weekdays and from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. at weekends. Your student card serves as your library card; please bring it with you when you enter. Books may be borrowed for thirty days and renewed once online. There are also group study rooms on the third floor, which can be reserved at the front desk.\n\nOn Friday afternoon, a one-hour guided tour will be given in English. Anyone interested is welcome to gather at the entrance at 2 p.m.\n\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the first picture, a father is lying on the sofa, smoking and watching television, while his little son sits beside him, imitating the same posture. In the second picture, the same father is reading at his desk, and the boy, holding a book of his own, follows his example quietly.\n\nThe two pictures tell a simple truth: example is more powerful than instruction. A child learns less from what his parents say than from what they do. If we tell him to read while we scroll through our phones, the words will carry no weight; if he sees us reading every evening, books will naturally become part of his world.\n\nThis truth extends far beyond the family. Teachers who arrive prepared and colleagues who keep their promises shape the people around them far more effectively than any slogan. We are, whether we like it or not, models for someone. Therefore the best way to lead is not to point at the path but to walk it, so that others may see where it leads."
 },
 "2017-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Professor Cook,\n\nWelcome to our city. Since you have just arrived, allow me to recommend two places that I believe you will enjoy.\n\nFirst, the old town along the river. It has kept its Ming-style houses and stone bridges, and a walk there in the late afternoon gives you a vivid sense of how the city looked four hundred years ago. Second, the city museum, which is only a twenty-minute bus ride from the campus. Its exhibition of local porcelain is small but beautifully arranged, and the labels are in both Chinese and English.\n\nIf you would like company, I would be happy to show you around this weekend.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the first picture, a young man sits in a comfortable chair in front of a bookshelf packed with volumes. Smug and satisfied, he says to himself, \"What a great number of books I have!\", yet he never opens any of them. In the second picture, another man sits at a plain desk with only a dozen books beside him, but he has drawn up a reading plan and is working through it page by page.\n\nThe contrast reveals the difference between possessing books and reading them. A shelf full of unopened volumes is merely furniture; it may create an illusion of learning, but it adds nothing to the mind. Reading, by contrast, is slow, patient work. It requires us to set a goal, to turn pages when we would rather do something else, and to think about what we have read.\n\nIn an age when information arrives in fragments, this lesson matters more than ever. We should not count how many books we own, but how many we have truly understood. A modest shelf that has been read is worth far more than a splendid library that has not."
 },
 "2018-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Professor,\n\nOn behalf of our university, I am writing to invite you to attend the graduation ceremony of the class of 2018, which will be held at 9 a.m. on 25 June in the Grand Hall.\n\nThe ceremony will last about two hours. There will be speeches by the president and by student representatives, the awarding of degrees, and a short performance by the music club. As a visiting expert who has worked with our students for three years, you are warmly welcome to sit on the platform and to present certificates to the graduates.\n\nPlease let us know before 10 June whether you can come, so that we can arrange seating and transport.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is shown in the picture, a student stands before a course-selection board, hesitating between two kinds of courses. On one side are the easy ones, famous for generous marks and light reading; on the other are the demanding ones, which require long hours in the library and may well end in a lower grade.\n\nThe picture mirrors a familiar choice on every campus. Many students quietly calculate which course will raise their average, and avoid whatever looks difficult. Yet a university is not a shop where credits are bought at the lowest price. Its value lies precisely in the courses that stretch us: the seminar that forces us to argue, the experiment that refuses to work, the reading list that seems endless. These are the experiences that change how we think.\n\nOf course, no one should deliberately fail. But when we choose comfort over challenge at every turn, we may graduate with a fine record and an untrained mind. Four years pass quickly; the easy road is soon forgotten, while the difficult one leaves something behind."
 },
 "2019-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Anna,\n\nThank you for your interest in our Aiding Rural Primary School project. Here are the details you asked about.\n\nThe project runs from 10 to 24 July in a village primary school in Gansu Province. Volunteers teach English, art and basic science to children aged six to twelve, about four hours a day. Accommodation and meals are provided by the school, and transport costs are partly covered by the university.\n\nWhat we need most is patience and a genuine wish to understand the children. No previous teaching experience is required; a short training session will be held before departure. If you would like to join us, please send me your application before 20 June.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the picture, two young men are climbing a mountain. One of them has sat down on the steps, exhausted, and says that he can go no further; the other stands beside him, holding out a hand and urging him to take just a few more steps.\n\nThe drawing captures a moment that everyone who has ever tried something difficult will recognise. Whether we are preparing for an examination, learning a language or building a career, there comes a point when progress slows and the summit seems no closer than before. At such moments, the temptation to stop is enormous, and the difference between those who arrive and those who turn back is often not talent but persistence.\n\nYet the picture also suggests something subtler: the climber is not alone. Encouragement from a companion can supply the strength that willpower alone cannot. In our own struggles, we should neither give up too early nor refuse the help that is offered. The road is long, but those who keep walking, and who walk together, will eventually reach the top."
 },
 "2020-y1": {
  "small": "Notice\n\nThe Students' Union is pleased to announce that the annual Singing Contest for international students will be held at 7 p.m. on Saturday, 28 December, in the university auditorium.\n\nThe contest consists of three rounds and is open to solo performers only. Those who wish to enter should submit a list of Chinese songs they would like to perform before 20 December; the judges will choose one song from each list for every round. Handsome prizes await the top ten winners.\n\nRegistration is open at our desk by the dining hall every day this week from 12 a.m. to 2 p.m. We look forward to your participation.\n\nThe Students' Union",
  "big": "In the left picture, a girl is concentrating on her assignments, determined to finish them as soon as possible. In stark contrast, the boy in the right picture is leaning back in his chair, with no intention of doing any schoolwork; in his own words, he is waiting until the last minute to make a start.\n\nThe two pictures illustrate the power of habit. What separates them is not intelligence but self-discipline, the ability to make ourselves do what ought to be done without being pushed by anyone else. Good habits turn effort into routine, protect us from distraction, and free our minds for the work that really matters. Bad ones do the opposite: they postpone, excuse and finally panic.\n\nThose who lack self-discipline often fail to get their priorities right. They rush through assignments or miss deadlines, which harms both their studies and their confidence. The remedy is not a heroic burst of willpower but a modest routine, repeated daily: a fixed hour for study, a phone left in another room, a task finished before it is due. Small habits, kept faithfully, decide large outcomes."
 },
 "2021-y1": {
  "small": "Dear John,\n\nI was delighted to hear that you have graduated and are thinking of working in China. Here are a few suggestions that may help.\n\nFirst, start learning Chinese now, even if your future job is in English. Daily conversation with colleagues will otherwise remain a barrier. Second, research the industry rather than the city; Chinese employers value relevant experience more than a famous address. Third, use campus career fairs and alumni networks, since many positions are filled through recommendation before they are advertised.\n\nRemember that the working culture here may differ from what you are used to, so be patient in your first months. If you need any help with your CV, just send it to me.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the picture, a young man in the full costume of Peking Opera stands on a stage, practising his part with great concentration. Below him, a group of onlookers are whispering and pointing, clearly amused by what they see. Yet the performer keeps his eyes on the empty seats in front of him, undisturbed.\n\nThe drawing speaks to everyone who has ever been laughed at for pursuing something he loves. Society has a way of prescribing what is sensible: a stable job, a familiar path, a respectable hobby. Anyone who steps outside those lines invites comment. The young man reminds us that such comment is cheap, while a dream that has been carefully chosen is not.\n\nTo persist, however, is not to be stubborn. It means listening to criticism, testing our abilities honestly, and then deciding, with open eyes, to continue. The difference between confidence and arrogance lies exactly there. Those who know why they are on the stage can bear a little laughter from the audience."
 },
 "2022-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Professor Brown,\n\nI am writing on behalf of the organising committee of the International Innovation Contest, which will be held at our university from 12 to 14 October.\n\nWe would be honoured if you could organise a team from your department to take part. Each team should consist of three to five students and one supervising professor, and should submit a proposal on the theme \"Technology for Everyday Life\" by 1 September. The contest includes a two-day design sprint, a poster session and a final presentation before a panel of judges.\n\nTravel and accommodation for participating teams will be covered by the host university. Please let me know if you are interested, and I will send you the detailed rules.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "In the first picture, a student sits alone at a desk piled with books on a single subject, his whole world narrowed to one narrow column of knowledge. In the second, another student stands at the meeting point of several fields, drawing on mathematics, art and computer science at once, and the ideas flow freely between them.\n\nThe contrast reminds us that the great questions of our time rarely respect the boundaries of a discipline. Artificial intelligence needs linguists as much as engineers; public health needs economists as much as doctors. A specialist who knows only his own field may solve the problems that have already been defined, but it is those who can cross borders who redefine them.\n\nFor students, this does not mean abandoning depth. It means refusing to treat the timetable as a wall. We can take a course outside our major, join a project from another faculty, or simply read beyond the syllabus. The wider the ground we stand on, the higher we can build."
 },
 "2023-y1": {
  "small": "Notice\n\nProfessor Smith is recruiting one student assistant for his research project on campus sports activities. The project examines how students use the university's sports facilities, and will last from March to June.\n\nThe assistant will help design questionnaires, collect data at the gym and the playground, and organise the results in spreadsheets. About six hours of work per week is expected. Applicants should be second- or third-year students, familiar with basic data analysis and able to communicate clearly with fellow students. Experience in survey work is an advantage but not a requirement.\n\nThose interested please send a short introduction to smithproject@university.edu.cn before 10 March. A brief interview will follow.\n\nLi Ming",
  "big": "As is depicted in the picture, a dragon boat race is under way on a river crowded with spectators. The boats cut through the water in perfect rhythm, the drummers beat time at the bow, and the banks are packed with people cheering, filming and waving flags.\n\nThe scene is more than a sporting event. The dragon boat festival has been celebrated for over two thousand years, yet it has never grown old. What keeps it alive is not simply tradition, but participation: ordinary people row the boats, cook the rice dumplings, and bring their children to watch. A heritage that is only displayed in museums slowly fades; a heritage that people practise every year grows stronger.\n\nThis is the lesson our traditional culture teaches us today. Protection does not mean putting the past behind glass. It means finding ways for old customs to enter modern life, whether through a race, a festival or a short video. When young people are willing to take up the oars, the boat will keep moving."
 },
 "2024-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Paul,\n\nGood to hear from you. An oral report on an ancient Chinese scientist is a fine topic, and here is how I would prepare.\n\nFirst, choose one figure rather than several. Zhang Heng, the Han-dynasty astronomer who built the seismoscope, or Li Shizhen, the Ming physician who compiled the Compendium of Materia Medica, would both work well. Second, pick two or three achievements and explain why they mattered at the time. Third, prepare one visual aid, a simple diagram or a portrait, so that your audience can follow you.\n\nRehearse aloud twice and keep the report within five minutes. If you send me your draft, I will be glad to comment on the language.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The picture shows a city park on a weekend morning: children running on the lawn, elderly people practising tai chi, and young couples walking along the lake. The table beside it tells a matching story. Between 2012 and 2022, the number of parks in Chinese cities rose steadily, while the green space per person also increased year by year.\n\nThese figures reflect a change in how we think about urban life. For decades, cities were judged mainly by the height of their buildings and the speed of their traffic. Parks were regarded as decoration, the first thing to be cut when money was short. Now they are understood as infrastructure, as necessary as roads and drains. A park cools the surrounding streets, absorbs rainwater, and gives people a place to meet their neighbours.\n\nThe change also answers a deeper need. In a fast-paced society, people increasingly look for somewhere to slow down. The growing number of parks is not only a matter of construction; it is a promise that a good city should be liveable, not merely productive."
 },
 "2025-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Paul,\n\nGlad you are interested! The craftsmen will come on 18 May and demonstrate three things: paper cutting, bamboo weaving and the making of clay figurines. Each of them will work at a small booth, so visitors can watch closely and try a simple piece themselves. In the afternoon there will be a short talk on how these crafts are being brought into modern design.\n\nAs for help, I could use two things. Could you take charge of the sign-up desk for the hands-on sessions? And if you have a camera, some photos for our poster would be wonderful.\n\nLet me know what suits you, and thank you for offering.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The table records the number of major durable consumer goods owned per hundred households in China between 2014 and 2023. Air conditioners rose most sharply, from 75.2 to 145.9, while washing machines and refrigerators grew more modestly, from 83.7 to 98.2 and from 85.5 to 103.4 respectively.\n\nSeveral forces lie behind these figures. The first is income growth: as families earn more, they replace necessities with goods that save labour and improve comfort. The second is infrastructure. Air conditioners could not spread without a stable power supply, and refrigerators could not reach every kitchen without reliable roads and cold chains. The third is the market itself, where competition has pushed prices down and quality up.\n\nIt is worth noticing which item grew fastest. Air conditioning is not a basic need in the way a washing machine is; its rapid growth shows that Chinese households are moving from \"having enough\" to \"living better\". This shift will continue, and it will shape demand for housing, energy and services in the years ahead."
 },
 "2026-y1": {
  "small": "Dear Paul,\n\nI am glad the letters moved you. They come from an exhibition I visited last month, which collected handwritten letters exchanged within ordinary Chinese families over the past fifty years.\n\nWhat struck me most was their ordinariness. One father writes to his daughter about the price of coal; a young couple exchange news of a new job in another province. Because they were never meant for publication, they preserve a warmth and honesty that few published memoirs can match.\n\nYes, they are on display at the City History Museum until the end of June, on the second floor. I would be happy to go with you if you come; the museum is free on Wednesday afternoons.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The two charts present a survey of public attitudes towards robots designed to care for the elderly. The first shows that a clear majority of respondents are willing to accept such robots in principle, with only a small proportion firmly opposed. The second lists the concerns people raised most often: safety and reliability come first, followed by cost, and then by the fear that machines may replace human company.\n\nRead together, the charts tell a nuanced story. People are not resisting technology out of prejudice; they are weighing it carefully. They welcome a device that can remind an elderly parent to take medicine, call for help after a fall, or ease the loneliness of a long afternoon. At the same time, they understand that care is not merely a set of tasks. Warmth, judgement and the sense of being loved cannot be programmed.\n\nThe implication is clear. Robots should be developed as assistants to families, not as substitutes for them. If designers focus on safety, keep prices reasonable, and leave room for human contact, these machines may genuinely improve the lives of a rapidly ageing population."
 },
 "2010-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Mr. Johnson,\n\nI have now returned to China, and I am writing to thank you for your warm reception during my stay in the United States.\n\nYour help made the exchange programme far more valuable than I had expected. The visits you arranged to local schools showed me a side of American education that no textbook could have taught, and the dinners at your home made me feel welcome in a foreign country.\n\nI would like to return the kindness. If you ever come to China, please let me know; I would be delighted to show you around my city and to introduce you to my colleagues. Our department would also be very glad to host you for a short lecture.\n\nWith warm regards,\nZhang Wei",
  "big": "The chart shows the rapid growth of mobile phone users in China between 2000 and 2008. In 2000 the number stood at roughly 85 million; by 2008 it had climbed to more than 600 million, an increase of several times in less than a decade. The curve rises steeply after 2003, and the upward trend shows no sign of flattening.\n\nSeveral factors account for this boom. Falling handset prices and cheaper call charges brought the device within reach of ordinary families, including farmers and students. Meanwhile, the expansion of the network into rural areas meant that a phone was no longer a luxury for city dwellers. Competition among operators also pushed down tariffs and improved service.\n\nThe social consequences are equally striking. Mobile phones have changed the way people work, shop and keep in touch, and they have given small businesses a direct line to their customers. Yet growth of this speed always brings problems: spam messages, privacy leaks and the digital divide between those who can afford the new technology and those who cannot. Managing these issues will be the task of the next decade."
 },
 "2011-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Li Ming,\n\nCongratulations on your admission to university! I was thrilled to hear the news, and I am sure you will do well there.\n\nLet me offer a few suggestions for the months ahead. First, learn to manage your time; nobody will remind you to get up for an eight o'clock class. Second, join a club or a sports team, which is the quickest way to make friends outside your major. Third, do not be afraid of the library; the habit of reading beyond the textbook will serve you for the rest of your life.\n\nEnjoy the summer first, and write to me when you have settled in. I look forward to hearing about your new life.\n\nYours,\nZhang Wei",
  "big": "The chart compares the market share of domestic and Japanese brands in a certain product category between 2008 and 2009. In 2008, Japanese brands held the larger share, around 35 percent, while domestic brands accounted for roughly 25 percent. Within a single year the situation reversed: domestic brands rose to nearly 40 percent, while Japanese brands fell to about 20 percent.\n\nWhat explains such a rapid shift? The most important factor is quality. Chinese manufacturers invested heavily in research and development, and the gap in reliability and design narrowed considerably. Price also mattered, especially during the financial crisis, when consumers became more careful about spending. Finally, domestic firms understood local needs better, offering after-sales service that foreign brands found hard to match.\n\nThe chart carries a broader lesson. Consumers are not loyal to a flag; they are loyal to value. A brand that rests on its reputation will lose ground to one that keeps improving. For Chinese enterprises, the rise shown here is encouraging, but it also raises the standard they must meet next year."
 },
 "2012-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to complain about an electronic dictionary I bought from your online store on 8 March, order number 20120308.\n\nWhen the device arrived, the screen flickered whenever the backlight was on, and three keys did not respond. I followed the instructions in the manual and reset the machine twice, but the problem remains. As a result, I cannot use it for my translation course, which begins next week.\n\nI would therefore ask you either to replace the dictionary with a new one or to refund the full amount. I have attached a copy of the receipt and a photograph of the faulty screen. I would appreciate a reply within seven days.\n\nYours faithfully,\nZhang Wei",
  "big": "The table presents a survey of employee satisfaction in a certain company. Staff were asked to rate five aspects of their work. Relations with colleagues received the highest score, followed by working environment; salary and opportunities for promotion came last, with training somewhere in between.\n\nThe pattern is revealing. It suggests that employees are reasonably happy with the people around them but dissatisfied with what the company offers in return for their effort. A pleasant atmosphere can keep a team together for a while, yet it cannot compensate for pay that lags behind the market or for a career that seems to go nowhere. When capable staff see no prospect of promotion, they either lose motivation or leave.\n\nThe findings point to clear priorities. The company should review its salary structure and make promotion criteria transparent, and it should invest in training so that employees can see a path forward. Improving these areas costs money, but losing experienced staff costs more. A satisfied workforce, the table reminds us, is built on fairness as much as on friendliness."
 },
 "2013-y2": {
  "small": "Notice\n\nA charity sale will be held by our class on Saturday, 15 June, from 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. on the square in front of the library. All the money raised will go to children in rural areas who cannot afford school supplies.\n\nWe are collecting items to sell: books, stationery, toys, and household goods in good condition. Please bring them to Room 302 before Thursday. On the day itself, we also need volunteers to run the stalls, keep accounts and help with packing.\n\nEveryone is welcome to come and buy something, or simply to drop by and show your support. For a child, a pencil and a notebook can be the beginning of a different future.\n\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows the results of a survey on part-time work among students in a certain university. About 45 percent of those surveyed have taken a part-time job at some point, while a little over 30 percent have never done so. Among those who work, the most common reasons are to earn pocket money and to gain experience, followed by the wish to meet new people.\n\nThe figures reflect a changing attitude towards work. A generation ago, students were expected to concentrate on their books, and a job during term time was seen as a distraction. Today, many regard part-time work as part of their education. It teaches punctuality, patience and the simple discipline of finishing what one has promised. It also gives students a realistic sense of what employers expect, which no lecture can supply.\n\nThere are risks, of course. Working too many hours may damage academic performance, and some jobs offer little more than repetitive labour. The sensible approach is to choose work that complements one's studies and to keep the balance in view. Part-time work is valuable, but only when it serves education rather than replaces it."
 },
 "2014-y2": {
  "small": "Dear John,\n\nI am glad that we will be sharing an apartment next term. Let me tell you a little about my living habits so that we can start off on the right foot.\n\nI usually get up around seven and go to bed before midnight. I like to cook at weekends, and I am happy to share whatever I make. I am fairly tidy, though my desk tends to be covered with books; I hope that will not bother you. I do not smoke, and I keep the music low when I study.\n\nI would be grateful for your advice: are there any rules in the building I should know, and where do students usually buy groceries? I look forward to meeting you.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows changes in China's urban and rural population over a period of twenty years. In the early years, the rural population exceeded the urban one by a wide margin. Year by year the two lines converged, until they crossed, after which the urban population continued to grow while the rural figure declined steadily.\n\nThis shift is the clearest expression of urbanisation. Rural migrants moved to cities for jobs, for schools and for hospitals, and their children grew up as city dwellers. The movement has lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty and made China the world's largest manufacturing base. It has also changed the countryside: many villages now have more empty houses than residents, and the people who remain are often elderly or very young.\n\nThe challenge for the coming years is balance. Cities need affordable housing, public transport and clean air; the countryside needs investment, modern farming and decent services so that people can choose to stay. Urbanisation should not mean abandoning one half of the country for the sake of the other."
 },
 "2015-y2": {
  "small": "Notice\n\nOur university will host a summer camp for high school students from 10 to 20 July, and volunteers are needed to help run it.\n\nCampers will take part in workshops on science, writing and public speaking, as well as team sports and a day trip to the city museum. Volunteers will act as group leaders, accompanying about ten students each, guiding them between activities and helping with any problems that arise.\n\nApplicants should be patient, energetic and able to work in a team. Fluency in English is an advantage. Meals and accommodation during the camp will be provided, and a certificate will be issued at the end.\n\nPlease send your name and contact details to camp@university.edu.cn before 30 June.\n\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The pie chart shows how residents of a certain city spent their money during the Spring Festival holiday. Food and gifts account for the largest share, followed by travel and entertainment, while spending on clothing and other items is comparatively small.\n\nThe distribution reflects the special character of the festival. Spring Festival is above all a family occasion: people buy ingredients for reunion dinners, take gifts to relatives, and travel long distances to be with their parents. It is not surprising, therefore, that food and gifts together make up nearly half of the total. The substantial share spent on travel also tells us something new: instead of staying at home for the whole holiday, more families now use the break to visit other cities or take a short tour.\n\nBehind these numbers lies a change in consumption habits. As incomes rise, holiday spending is shifting from necessities towards experience. People are willing to pay for a good meal, a comfortable trip and time shared with those they love. The Spring Festival economy, in this sense, is a mirror of a better-off society."
 },
 "2016-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Jack,\n\nThank you for your kind words about the translation contest. Your congratulations mean a great deal to me.\n\nSince you asked for advice, here is what helped me most. First, read the Chinese passage twice before writing anything, so that you understand its tone as well as its content. Second, translate sentence by sentence, but revise paragraph by paragraph; the flow matters more than word-for-word accuracy. Third, keep a notebook of expressions you meet in good translations, and try to use them yourself. Finally, read your draft aloud; awkward sentences usually reveal themselves to the ear.\n\nI am happy to look at your practice pieces if you send them to me. Good luck!\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart presents a survey of university students' purposes for travel. Gaining experience and broadening horizons is the most frequently chosen reason, followed by relieving pressure and appreciating natural scenery. Meeting new people and shopping come further down the list.\n\nThe results suggest that travel has become part of education rather than a mere leisure activity. Students are not primarily looking for luxury or entertainment; they want to see how other people live, to test themselves in unfamiliar situations, and to return with a wider sense of the world. In this light, a journey is a form of learning that no classroom can replace.\n\nThe second most common answer is equally telling. Academic pressure is heavy, and a few days away offers a pause that restores both body and mind. Universities would do well to notice this: instead of treating travel as time taken away from study, they might recognise it as part of a student's development, and make it easier through flexible schedules and affordable programmes."
 },
 "2017-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Professor Williams,\n\nThank you for your invitation. I would be delighted to give a presentation on Chinese culture to your international students.\n\nMy talk will have three parts. First, I will introduce the idea of \"family\" in Chinese life, explaining why the Spring Festival reunion dinner matters so much. Second, I will show a few examples of how traditional art, such as paper cutting and calligraphy, still appears in modern design. Finally, I will leave ten minutes for questions, and I would like to invite the students to compare these customs with their own.\n\nThe presentation will take about forty minutes. Please let me know the date and the size of the audience, and I will prepare the materials accordingly.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows the number of museums and the number of visits in China between 2013 and 2015. Both figures rose, but not at the same pace: the number of museums increased modestly, while visits grew far more quickly, so that the average number of visits per museum also climbed.\n\nThis gap is worth explaining. It suggests that the growth in visits was driven not simply by new buildings but by a change in public interest. Free admission, better exhibitions and improved transport have made museums accessible to families, students and elderly people who rarely visited them before. Social media has also played a part, turning a well-designed exhibition into a place worth photographing and sharing.\n\nThe trend carries a message for cultural institutions. Building a museum is easy; filling it with meaning is not. If the rise in visitors is to continue, museums must keep renewing their displays, explain their collections in language that ordinary people understand, and offer programmes for children. Culture becomes alive only when it is visited, discussed and remembered."
 },
 "2018-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Professor Smith,\n\nI am very sorry to tell you that I will not be able to visit you next Monday as we arranged. My supervisor has just asked me to attend an urgent field survey in another city, and I will be away from 12 to 16 May.\n\nI apologise for the short notice and for any inconvenience this may cause. The questions I wanted to discuss are not urgent, and I would be glad to meet you at any time that suits you after 20 May. Alternatively, we could talk online if that is more convenient.\n\nThank you for your understanding.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows what consumers in a certain city consider most important when choosing a restaurant. Food quality comes first, chosen by nearly two thirds of respondents, followed by the dining environment and price. Service and reputation rank lower, while location matters least.\n\nThe results reveal a demanding but sensible public. Diners are no longer satisfied with a full plate; they want a meal that tastes good, in a place that feels comfortable, at a price that seems fair. The high ranking of environment also reflects a change in social habits: eating out has become a way of meeting friends and marking occasions, so the setting matters as much as the food.\n\nFor restaurant owners, the message is straightforward. Advertising and discounts may attract a first visit, but only quality brings people back. Investing in ingredients, in the training of cooks and in the design of the dining room is therefore more profitable in the long run than spending heavily on promotion."
 },
 "2019-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Professor Smith,\n\nThank you for asking me to plan the debate on city traffic. I would like to suggest the topic \"Should private cars be restricted in city centres?\"\n\nThis question is timely and genuinely arguable. Supporters can point to congestion, air pollution and the space taken up by parking; opponents can argue about personal freedom, the needs of the disabled and the weakness of public transport in some districts. Both sides have strong evidence, which should make for a lively discussion.\n\nI have booked Room 204 for Thursday, 16 May, from 7 to 9 p.m. The debate will have two teams of four, a ten-minute opening statement from each side, a free-debate period of thirty minutes, and closing remarks. I will send the participants the rules and reading materials a week in advance.\n\nYours sincerely,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows the number of museums in a certain city between 2013 and 2018. The figure rose steadily from year to year, with the sharpest increase occurring in the middle of the period, and the total nearly doubled by 2018.\n\nSuch growth is a sign of public investment in culture. As incomes rise, people expect more from their city than roads and shops; they want places where history is preserved and where children can see what cannot be found in a textbook. Many of the new museums are small and specialised, devoted to local crafts, to industry or to a single artist, which makes them especially close to the communities they serve.\n\nBuilding, however, is only the beginning. A museum that opens and then stands unchanged will soon be empty. The real test is whether it can attract visitors year after year through good exhibitions, reasonable opening hours and thoughtful interpretation. The numbers in the chart are encouraging, but the work of filling these buildings with life has only just begun."
 },
 "2020-y2": {
  "small": "Dear friends,\n\nI am writing to give you some information about our tour of the old town on Saturday.\n\nThe site is a well-preserved water town about forty minutes from the campus. Its stone bridges, narrow lanes and wooden houses date back to the Ming dynasty, and a few families still live along the canal. We will walk through the main street, visit the old pawnshop and take a boat ride in the afternoon.\n\nA few suggestions: wear comfortable shoes, since the paving stones are uneven; bring some cash, as many small shops do not accept cards; and please keep your voice low near the houses, because people live there.\n\nWe will meet at the north gate at 8 a.m. See you then.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart presents a survey of how students in a certain university obtain information. The Internet is by far the most common channel, used by the overwhelming majority, while traditional media such as newspapers and radio are chosen by only a small minority. Face-to-face communication with teachers and classmates falls somewhere in between.\n\nThe figures confirm what we see every day. A student who wants to know what happened an hour ago will reach for a phone rather than a newspaper; a question about an assignment is more likely to be answered in a group chat than in a library. Speed and convenience have made the screen the default doorway to the world.\n\nYet the survey also hints at a loss. Information that arrives instantly is often fragmentary, unchecked and quickly forgotten. Reading a newspaper or a book, by contrast, forces us to follow an argument to its end. The ideal is not to reject the new but to combine it with the old: use the Internet to find, and use careful reading to understand."
 },
 "2021-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Jack,\n\nI would like to invite you to join our online meeting on Friday, 12 June, at 3 p.m. Beijing time.\n\nThe meeting is organised by our student association and will focus on how international students can make the most of campus resources. Two librarians and a careers adviser will speak, and there will be time for questions. It will be held on the Tencent Meeting platform, and the link will be sent to you a day in advance.\n\nAll you need is a computer with a microphone and a stable connection. If the time does not suit you, the recording will be available afterwards.\n\nPlease let me know if you can come, and feel free to invite a friend.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows how residents of a certain city prefer to exercise. Walking and jogging are the most popular choices by a wide margin, followed by ball games and cycling. Swimming, fitness classes and other activities attract smaller groups.\n\nThe pattern is easy to understand. Walking costs nothing, requires no equipment and fits into any schedule; it can be done on the way to work, in a park after dinner, or with a friend. Ball games appeal for a different reason, namely the pleasure of competition and company. Expensive gym memberships, by contrast, attract only a dedicated minority.\n\nThe findings have practical value. If the aim is to encourage more people to exercise, the city should invest in what people already do: safe pavements, well-lit parks, and free outdoor facilities. Rather than building another costly stadium, it may be wiser to make the ordinary walk pleasant. Physical health, after all, is built in daily habits rather than in occasional efforts."
 },
 "2022-y2": {
  "small": "Dear friends,\n\nI am writing to invite you to our campus food festival, which will take place on Saturday, 21 May, from 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. on the square in front of the library.\n\nMore than thirty stalls will offer dishes from different regions of China, from spicy Sichuan noodles to sweet Cantonese cakes. There will also be a cooking demonstration at noon and a small competition in which students make dumplings in teams. Vegetarian options will be available at most stalls.\n\nYou are warmly welcome to come and taste, and if you would like to cook something from your own country, we would be delighted to give you a stall. Just tell me before 15 May.\n\nLooking forward to seeing you there!\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows changes in the per capita income of urban and rural residents in China between 2018 and 2020. Both rose steadily, and the gap between them did not widen; in relative terms it narrowed slightly, as rural income grew a little faster than urban income.\n\nThis is an encouraging result. For many years the income gap was one of the country's most stubborn problems. The narrowing shown here reflects several policies: the removal of agricultural taxes, investment in rural roads and broadband, and support for e-commerce that allows farmers to sell directly to consumers. Remittances from migrant workers also continue to support families in the countryside.\n\nThe chart nevertheless reminds us how much remains to be done. Rural income is still well below urban income in absolute terms, and averages conceal wide differences between regions. To close the gap further, what is needed is not simply higher prices for farm produce, but better education, healthcare and public services in rural areas. Income equality is achieved by building opportunity, not by transferring money alone."
 },
 "2023-y2": {
  "small": "Dear David,\n\nYou asked which event is worth your Sunday, and I would suggest the art exhibition.\n\nMy reason is simple: the exhibition is temporary, while robot shows tour the country all year round. This one brings together paintings from a private collection that is rarely open to the public, including several works by Qi Baishi. It is also held in the old railway station, a building worth seeing in itself.\n\nThat said, if you are more interested in technology, the robot show is not a bad choice; I hear there is a demonstration of household robots every hour. Either way, let me know, and I would be happy to go with you.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows the level of health literacy among Chinese residents from 2012 to 2021. The proportion rose steadily throughout the period, roughly doubling over the decade, though it still accounts for only a minority of the population.\n\nThe growth reflects a real change in how people think about health. A generation ago, illness was something to be treated after it appeared; today, more people understand that diet, exercise and regular check-ups matter more than medicine. Public campaigns, health columns in newspapers and short videos on social media have all contributed, and community clinics now offer lectures that reach ordinary families.\n\nThe remaining gap is the more important message. Health literacy is unevenly distributed: it is higher among city dwellers and the well-educated than among rural residents and the elderly, precisely the groups who need it most. If the next decade is to continue this progress, health education must be brought to village squares and to the screens that older people actually use."
 },
 "2024-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Jack,\n\nHere is my plan for our survey on the protection of old houses in the ancient town. Please tell me what you think.\n\nI suggest we focus on the street behind the main temple, where about twenty families still live in Ming-style houses. We could prepare a short questionnaire for the residents, asking how long they have lived there, what repairs their houses need, and whether they would accept government funding in exchange for keeping the original structure. We should also photograph ten houses and note their condition. Two days on site, I think, would be enough.\n\nWould a weekend in early April suit you? And do you think we should interview the town's heritage office as well?\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows what students gained most from a labour practice course at a certain university. Practical skills and a better understanding of ordinary work rank highest, followed by teamwork and a stronger sense of responsibility; improved communication comes next, while a few students mention little benefit.\n\nThe results justify the place of labour practice in higher education. For years, many students moved from classroom to classroom, learning theories about society without ever joining it. A few weeks of real work, whether in a workshop, a farm or a community clinic, changes that. Students discover that a task must be finished by a certain hour, that a colleague depends on them, and that a job done badly has consequences.\n\nThis is what the chart is really measuring: not skills alone, but the growth of a working attitude, which no examination can test. Universities should therefore treat such courses as a serious part of the curriculum, not as an interlude before the holidays. The experience may be tiring, but it teaches something that lasts."
 },
 "2025-y2": {
  "small": "Dear John,\n\nI am planning a short play based on a classic Chinese novel, and I would like you to join us.\n\nWe have chosen a chapter from Journey to the West, in which the Monkey King disguises himself to protect his master. The play will last about twenty minutes and will be performed in English at the campus theatre in late May. Rehearsals will be on Wednesday evenings, and no previous acting experience is needed.\n\nI think you would be excellent as the Monkey King: the part needs energy, humour and a good deal of improvisation, all of which suit you. If you would rather try something else, we also need someone for lighting and music.\n\nDo let me know by Friday, and I will send you the script.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart shows the main daily leisure activities of elderly people in a certain community. Watching television is by far the most common, chosen by over ninety percent, followed by walking. Gardening and reading attract about a third, while chess and similar games are taken up by fewer than one in five.\n\nThe pattern is understandable but also revealing. Television is cheap, requires little effort and fills long hours, especially for those who live alone. Walking, the second choice, is the simplest form of exercise and often the only social occasion of the day. The relatively low figures for reading and chess suggest that many older people lack either the eyesight, the company or the confidence to take up activities that demand more.\n\nThe community can respond to this. Reading groups with large-print books, chess tables in the courtyard, and gardening plots on the roof would cost little and could turn solitary habits into shared ones. The aim is not to make the elderly give up television, but to ensure that their day contains more than a screen."
 },
 "2026-y2": {
  "small": "Dear Jack,\n\nThank you for sharing the video of your family's trip to China. I watched it twice, and it made me smile both times.\n\nWhat I liked most was not the famous sights but the small moments: your father trying to order noodles with gestures, your sister laughing at the wrong bus stop, and the whole family crowded around a pot of hot pot. It reminded me that travel is remembered through such details rather than through photographs of monuments.\n\nI am curious about several things. Which city did you enjoy most, and why? Did you manage to travel by high-speed train? And what did your parents think of the food in the south compared with the north?\n\nIf you have more clips, please send them. And do consider coming again; I would be glad to show you my hometown.\n\nYours,\nLi Ming",
  "big": "The chart presents a survey of parents' views on children's outdoor activities. A clear majority agree that time spent outdoors is important for a child's growth, and many report that their children spend less time outside than they did at the same age. The most frequently mentioned obstacles are heavy schoolwork, safety concerns and the attraction of electronic devices.\n\nThe findings describe a paradox of modern childhood. Parents know that running in a park, climbing a tree or simply getting muddy is good for both body and mind, yet they find it difficult to arrange. Homework and after-school classes fill the daylight hours, while busy streets and anxious news reports make many parents reluctant to let children go out alone.\n\nSolving this requires more than individual effort. Schools could reduce the burden of assignments, neighbourhoods could provide safe playgrounds within walking distance, and communities could organise weekend activities supervised by volunteers. Outdoor play is not a luxury to be fitted in when everything else is done; it is part of growing up, and it deserves a proper place in a child's day."
 }
};
